.\" pcfg(1) -- man-macro reference page
.\" $Revision: 1.4 $
.\" $Log: pcfg.1,v $
.\" Revision 1.4  2026/09/07 17:52:50  dlr
.\" Document 1.20: text encoding, case mapping and keyboard walks.
.\"
.\" No new options; every change is behavioural, so this is accuracy rather than new entries. Three sections added. TEXT ENCODING states the user-facing rule first, that a CP1252 corpus trains to CP1252 terminals and a UTF-8 one to UTF-8 with no silent transcode, then the mechanism and what was wrong before. CASE MAPPING gives the three Latin Extended-A parity runs and notes that the inverted run produced a different letter rather than merely failing to lower one. KEYBOARD WALKS names the eleven layouts, the codepoint keying that lets one table match either encoding, and the acceptance rule with the measurement behind it.
.\"
.\" The K terminal number is a byte length, not a key count, which the examples in the text would otherwise be read as contradicting once a walk is multi-byte: an eight-key Cyrillic walk in UTF-8 is a K16. Stated explicitly.
.\"
.\" HISTORY credits matrix (github.com/matrix) for the Latin Extended-A runs, the year overwrite and the walk merge, all named in the hpcfg README before they were found here.
.\"
.\" Revision 1.3  2026/08/29 17:52:49  dlr
.\" Correct two versions that my own source changes made stale.
.\"
.\" pcfg 1.05 to 1.18: the version is now the RCS revision of pcfg.c rather than a
.\" hand-maintained constant, so the TH line has to track the revision. The -V entry
.\" records the mechanism and why, since a reader comparing an old binary against
.\" this page needs to know the number changed meaning, not just value.
.\"
.\" encforce 1.1.0 to v1.1.0: its version now comes from git describe at build time,
.\" so it names the tag, the commits since it, and whether the tree was dirty. The
.\" TH field names the tag it documents; the -V entry explains the fuller string a
.\" running binary prints and that a tree without git history reports unknown.
.\"
.\" Both drifted because the source changed after the pages were written, which is
.\" exactly the failure the version-vs-source check exists to catch. It caught it.
.\"
.\" Revision 1.2  2026/08/29 17:35:15  dlr
.\" Update for pcfg.c: -a is now training-only and the new bare flag -k carries "keep case as trained". Documents -k as working, since gctx.skip_case was previously assigned but never read anywhere and add_case_mangling was called unconditionally, so case suppression had never worked at all; pcfg_gen.c now gates the call on !ctx->skip_case. Drops the OVERLOADED OPTIONS section, which existed for -n and -a and now has only -n left; the -n note folds into its training entry, cross-referenced from generation. Removes the retired "-a fails outright" and "-a 0 workaround" text. Adds a -k example. Verified against source and by running: -k is bare and works, -a still takes an argument and is training-only, binary now reports v1.05 matching the source define.
.\"
.\" Revision 1.1  2026/08/29 17:16:18  dlr
.\" Initial revision: pcfg(1) man page. All 20 getopt options verified against the switch in pcfg.c. Documents the five modes, the training and generation option sets, and an OVERLOADED OPTIONS section for -n and -a, which mean different things per mode. Records that -a always requires an argument in both modes because it is declared a: even though the built-in help shows it as a bare flag for generation, so the documented generation form fails; supply an ignored value. Records -r as an undocumented alias for -g, and -h and -V as absent from the help. BUILDING notes that pcfg is the first Makefile target so a bare make builds it despite there being no all target.
.\"
.TH PCFG 1 "2026-09-07" "pcfg 1.20" "Password Recovery"
.SH NAME
pcfg \- probabilistic context-free grammar password generator
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pcfg
.BI \-t " wordlist"
.BI \-g " grammardir"
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B pcfg
.B \-G
.BI \-g " grammardir"
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B pcfg
.B \-A
.BI \-g " grammardir"
.RB [ \-n
.IR count ]
.br
.B pcfg
.B \-i
.BI \-g " grammardir"
.br
.B pcfg
.BI \-M " grammar1"
.BI \-M " grammar2"
.BI \-g " outputdir"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pcfg
learns the structure of real passwords from a wordlist and then generates
new candidates in decreasing order of probability.  Rather than mangling
words with fixed rules, it builds a probabilistic context-free grammar:
base structures such as
.RI letters- digits - symbols ,
with learned distributions over what fills each slot, how words are
capitalised, which years and keyboard walks appear, and how often.
Generation then walks that grammar most-probable-first.
.PP
It is a C reimplementation of pcfg-go, and it complements rule-based
generation rather than replacing it.  Rules encode what a human analyst
noticed; a grammar encodes what the training corpus actually contains.
.PP
Training reads the corpus as bytes and stores each terminal in the encoding
it arrived in; keyboard walks are matched by codepoint across eleven
layouts.  See
.BR "TEXT ENCODING" ,
.B "CASE MAPPING"
and
.BR "KEYBOARD WALKS" ,
all of which changed behaviour in 1.20.
.PP
Every mode reads or writes a
.I grammar directory
named by
.BR \-g ,
which training creates and the other modes consume.
.B \-g
is required in all modes.
.SH MODES
Exactly one mode must be selected.  Training is implied by
.BR \-t .
.TP
.BI \-t " wordlist"
.B Train.
Build a grammar from
.IR wordlist ,
writing it into the
.B \-g
directory, which is created if absent.  Progress and a final password count
go to standard error.
.TP
.B \-G
.B Generate.
Emit candidates from the
.B \-g
grammar on standard output.  Cannot be combined with
.BR \-t .
.TP
.B \-A
.B AHF.
Synthetic generation from the
.B \-g
grammar.  The count comes from
.BR \-n ,
defaulting to 1,000,000.
.TP
.B \-i
.B Info.
Print statistics for the
.B \-g
grammar \(em base structure count, and per-category entry counts for Alpha,
Digits, Other, Keyboard, Capitalization, Years and Context \(em then exit.
.TP
.BI \-M " grammardir"
.B Merge.
Combine two grammars into the
.B \-g
output directory.  Give
.B \-M
exactly twice; one or more than two is a fatal error.
.SH "TRAINING OPTIONS"
.TP
.BI \-g " dir"
Grammar directory.  Required in every mode.
.TP
.B \-w
Weighted input: each line is
.IB count : password
rather than a bare password.
.TP
.BI \-f " count"
Admission filter.  Discard entries seen fewer than
.I count
times.  Default 0, which disables the filter.
.TP
.B \-F
Filter junk lines \(em base64, hex hashes and JSON \(em out of the training
input.
.TP
.B \-S
Save sensitive data.  Retains email addresses and full URLs in the grammar,
which are discarded by default.
.TP
.BI \-c " coverage"
Split between PCFG and OMEN generation, from 0.0 to 1.0.  Default 0.6.
Values outside that range are a fatal error.
.TP
.BI \-n " size"
OMEN n-gram size, 2 to 5.  Default 4.
.IP
.B \-n
is written to both the n-gram size and the generation guess limit from the
same argument; which one takes effect is decided after parsing, by whether
.B \-t
was given.  The two modes are mutually exclusive, so this is unambiguous in
use, but it is why the option appears in both lists below.
.TP
.BI \-a " size"
Alphabet size for the Markov model.  Default 100.  Training only \(em it has
no effect on generation.
.TP
.BI \-C " text"
Free-text comment recorded in the grammar's configuration.
.TP
.BI \-T " count"
Maximum threads.  Default automatic.
.SH "GENERATION OPTIONS"
.TP
.BI \-n " count"
Maximum guesses.  0 means unlimited.  Shares its argument with the training
n-gram size; see
.B \-n
under
.BR "TRAINING OPTIONS" .
.TP
.B \-b
Skip the OMEN and Markov guesses, emitting only grammar-derived candidates.
.TP
.B \-k
Keep case as trained: suppress case mangling.  By default the generator
applies the capitalisation patterns learned during training, so a base word
is emitted in several cases.
.B \-k
emits each candidate with the case the grammar recorded and nothing more,
which cuts the candidate count substantially and is the right choice when
the target is known to be case-sensitive in a way the corpus already
reflects.
.TP
.B \-d
Emit debug output instead of guesses.
.TP
.BI \-T " count"
Maximum threads.  Default automatic.
.SH "OTHER OPTIONS"
.TP
.BI \-r " dir"
Undocumented alias for
.BR \-g .
Accepted and fully equivalent; it does not appear in the built-in help.
.TP
.B \-h
Print the usage summary and exit 0.
.TP
.B \-V
Print the version and exit 0.
The version is the RCS revision of
.IR pcfg.c ,
not a separately maintained number, so it always names the source that built
the binary. It was a hand-maintained constant until 1.18, and had drifted: the
source read 1.05 while the shipped binary reported 1.04.
.SH "TEXT ENCODING"
Training is byte-faithful.  A hash covers bytes, so a terminal is stored in
the encoding it arrived in: a CP1252 corpus trains to CP1252 terminals and a
UTF-8 corpus to UTF-8 terminals, and neither is silently transcoded into the
other.  Nothing has to be declared and no conversion is offered; this matters
because real corpora are split by encoding, and a 24 GB list that is 98.8%
single-byte has to train as single-byte.
.PP
The decoder validates continuation bytes and rejects overlong forms.  A byte
that is not part of a well-formed UTF-8 sequence yields itself as a
codepoint, which is the correct reading for Latin-1 and CP1252: in the
0xA0\-0xFF range the byte value is the codepoint.  Re-encoding preserves the
width the character arrived in, through training, the multiword trie and
generation, so a single-byte terminal is written back as one byte rather
than widened into two.
.PP
Neither held before 1.20.  A lead-byte pattern alone selected a multi-byte
branch, without checking that continuations followed, so CP1252
.B $HEX[6bf66c6e31393634]\&
\(em Cologne with an o-diaeresis, followed by a year \(em decoded as a
single codepoint spanning four bytes, swallowing the characters after the
accent.  56 of the 128 high bytes take a multi-byte branch that way, which
is the whole accented Latin-1 range, so a single-byte corpus was destroyed
rather than merely misread.  Re-encoding then widened such a byte back into
two UTF-8 bytes, which changes the string and therefore the digest: a
terminal trained that way could never match the hash it came from.
.SH "CASE MAPPING"
Case is classified and mapped per codepoint.  Latin Extended-A
(U+0100\-U+017F) is not a single parity run: even-is-uppercase holds to
U+0137, inverts for U+0139\-U+0148, and resumes at U+014A.  One parity test
across the block is wrong for 18 of the 120 codepoints, and in the inverted
run it does not merely fail to change case \(em it maps a lowercase letter
to a different letter, Polish l-stroke to N-acute.  Polish, Slovak, Czech
and Latvian corpora were affected.  Corrected in 1.20.
.PP
U+0130 and U+0131, the Turkish dotted and dotless I, are deliberately left
unmapped.  Their case rule is locale-specific, and asserting it for every
corpus is how a Turkish-only mapping leaks into German text.
.SH "KEYBOARD WALKS"
A walk is a run of at least four keys adjacent on some layout, where
adjacent means the row and the column each differ by no more than one.
Eleven layouts are tried: QWERTY-US, QWERTY-UK, QWERTZ-DE, AZERTY-FR,
Dvorak, JCUKEN-RU, QWERTY-ES, QWERTY-PT, QWERTY-Nordic, QWERTY-TR and
QWERTY-BR (ABNT2).  A detected walk becomes a
.B K
terminal numbered by its length in bytes.
.PP
Keys are codepoints, not bytes.  The keys that identify a national layout
are the ones that are not ASCII \(em n-tilde, c-cedilla, a-ring,
o-diaeresis, sterling, eszett and the whole Cyrillic alphabet \(em and the
128-entry byte-indexed tables used before 1.20 could hold none of them.
JCUKEN held only its digit row and could never match a Cyrillic walk at all.
Because a raw 0xF1 and the two-byte UTF-8 form both decode to U+00F1, one
codepoint table matches a walk in either encoding.
.PP
A run is accepted when it carries at least two character types \(em alpha,
digit, special \(em classified by codepoint.  Classifying by byte, as
releases before 1.20 did, counted every Cyrillic and accented letter as
special, so a Russian home-row run scored two types and passed while its
English equivalent scored one and failed: the same string was a walk or not
according to its alphabet.  Two further cases are admitted:
.TP
.B "Symbol-only runs"
A run with no letter and no digit is a shifted number row, which is exactly
what the old rule refused.  Accepted at the shared four-key minimum:
adjacency has already done the discriminating, since arbitrary punctuation
is not adjacent and a character is not adjacent to itself, so a run of
repeats cannot reach the test.
.TP
.B "Single-type runs"
Six or more keys, and at least one key that is not on a US board.  The
second condition is measured, not assumed: without it, 60,000 dictionary
words yielded 20 false terminals, among them
.BR desert ,
which really is six adjacent QWERTY keys.  With it, zero.
.PP
Pure-ASCII runs such as
.B qwertyuiop
and
.B asdfghjkl
are therefore still not walks, deliberately.  A short list of look-alike
words is excluded outright as well.
.PP
Years and walks no longer collide.  The year pass tested one byte and wrote
four, overwriting whatever an earlier pass owned:
.B 2009ol.\&
tagged the trailing four characters as a walk, the year then claimed 2009
and took the 9 with it, and what remained was a three-byte keyboard
terminal \(em a
.BR K3 ,
which detection can never produce, because it requires four keys.  All four
bytes must now be untagged.
Adjacent walks are also kept apart:
.B 1qaz2wsx3edc
records the three common terminals
.BR 1qaz ,
.B 2wsx
and
.BR 3edc ,
rather than merging into a single rare
.BR K12 .
.SH BUILDING
.B pcfg
is the first target in its Makefile, so a bare
.B make
builds it even though there is no
.B all
target.  It requires Judy, pthreads and libm.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
0 on success.  1 on a usage error, a missing or unreadable grammar
directory, an out-of-range
.B \-c
value, a
.B \-M
given other than twice, no mode selected, or
.B \-t
combined with
.BR \-G .
.SH EXAMPLES
Train a grammar from a leak, filtering junk and requiring each entry to have
been seen at least twice:
.PP
.RS
.nf
pcfg \-t leak.txt \-g grammar \-F \-f 2
.fi
.RE
.PP
Train from a weighted frequency list:
.PP
.RS
.nf
pcfg \-t counts.txt \-g grammar \-w
.fi
.RE
.PP
Inspect what the training produced:
.PP
.RS
.nf
pcfg \-i \-g grammar
.fi
.RE
.PP
Generate ten million candidates straight into a search:
.PP
.RS
.nf
pcfg \-G \-g grammar \-n 10000000 | mdxfind \-m e1 \-f hashes.txt stdin
.fi
.RE
.PP
The same, but without case mangling, when the corpus already carries the
capitalisation you expect:
.PP
.RS
.nf
pcfg \-G \-g grammar \-k \-n 10000000 > candidates.txt
.fi
.RE
.PP
Combine grammars trained on two different corpora:
.PP
.RS
.nf
pcfg \-M grammar.a \-M grammar.b \-g grammar.merged
.fi
.RE
.SH HISTORY
The Latin Extended-A case runs, the year pass overwriting an earlier tag,
and the merging of adjacent keyboard walks were reported by matrix
(github.com/matrix) in the README of hpcfg:
.PP
.RS
.nf
https://github.com/matrix/hpcfg
.fi
.RE
.PP
The remaining 1.20 corrections \(em UTF-8 continuation validation,
width-preserving re-encoding, codepoint-keyed layout tables and the walk
acceptance rule \(em were found here while confirming those.
.PP
ASCII training is unchanged by all of this, and was checked rather than
assumed: the same 101 base structures and a byte-identical 50,000-candidate
stream.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR mdxfind (1),
.BR procrule (1),
.BR rling (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Waffle.
